			"text": "The Settings button at the right end of the app bar ( ) opens Settings. Profile page: your KAT Management site credentials, your Conduent network account, and a TFS Personal Access Token (PAT). Display page: adjust the appearance and layout of the application - theme, density, how the rail collapses, whether Releases and Sentinel are one page or several - and when CalcEngine checkouts and What's Happening items change. Work Items page: configure your work item queries and workflow used within the application. Grazer page: configure local search roots, exclusions, result limits, ZIP/Git options, and replacement confirmation. Repository Status page: choose the folders searched for Git repositories, the ignore patterns, how often status is checked and remotes are fetched, and which Git actions ask for confirmation. Connections page: manage your application's connections to external services and data sources. Folder paths in Grazer, the Repository Status roots and the Release folder setting share an editable picker. Type or paste a path, use the up/down arrows and Enter to choose one of up to 25 immediate child-folder suggestions, or press the folder button beside the path; Esc closes the suggestions. These edits stay in the Settings draft until you choose Save; Cancel discards them. Editing a path does not run a Grazer search or rescan repositories; saving changed Repository Status roots does. Notable Features On first launch with no PAT, Settings opens on Profile by itself. Nothing asks for credentials in the middle of a refresh. A section whose sign-in is missing or rejected says Needs credentials; fix it on Profile and that section refreshes. Changes on Display preview immediately in Settings and the dashboard behind it. You can mix theme families: a light preference does not change your dark preference. For the keyboard warriors, Ctrl+Enter triggers an immediate save in the Settings dialog. Validation errors are indicated with a badge count beside its page in the list, so a problem on a page you are not looking at is still easy to find. Saved settings reach the open dashboard immediately; no refresh is needed. When a newer build is waiting, an Update available button appears at the bottom of the rail. Pressing it replaces the app and restarts it, so finish what you are doing first. Show release notes, under the page list, opens What's New. Open settings file opens the saved settings file itself."
