			"heading": "Searching",
			"text": "Type a literal term in Search term and press Enter to add it as a chip; add up to 100. A search term can be up to 512 characters, and replacement text a single line of up to 4,096. A line matches when it contains any term (OR matching); terms are not regular expressions. New chips ignore case and match substrings. Each chip has compact buttons for Match case (Aa) and Match whole word (underlined ab, like VS Code); a highlighted button means the option is on. Whole-word matching requires neither end of the literal term to touch a Unicode letter, digit, combining mark or connector punctuation such as an underscore. For example, cat matches (cat) but not cats or cat_name. The same text can appear more than once only with different matching settings. The red trash button removes a chip. File patterns are wildcards matched against filenames, separated by semicolons, commas or line breaks. Choose a named root or All, then select a result mode: Line Matches shows matching lines, their line numbers and which terms matched. Click a line number to open the file in VS Code. ZIP-entry line numbers are informational because archive entries are read-only. File Names Only groups matching files and reports their matching-line counts and terms. Folder Names Only summarizes matching folders, files, lines and terms. Search submits the chips and filters. Text still sitting in Search term is added as a chip first. While a search runs, Search is replaced by Cancel and every other input, chip and action is disabled. F5 reruns the last submitted search, not edits made afterward; before the first accepted search, F5 does nothing. Clear Results clears the results. See Keyboard Shortcuts for refresh behavior on other pages."
